    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That 6-8% range makes sense. I'll check my meter again. I'm also wondering about the tool's edge profile. I've been using a standard flat bevel, but some people suggest a slightly micro-bevel on the tip for extra durability. Would that help prevent the edge from rolling when I'm hitting those harder sections of the grain, or would it just make the tool more prone to glancing off?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The edge profile of your chisel is indeed a crucial factor in achieving a clean, controlled cut. A micro-bevel on the tip of the chisel can be beneficial in certain situations, but it's not a one-size-fits-all solution.\n\nA micro-bevel can help prevent the edge from rolling or digging in too deeply, especially when cutting through harder sections of the grain. This is because the micro-bevel creates a more acute angle between the edge and the workpiece, allowing the chisel to \"dig in\" more smoothly and maintain a consistent cutting action.\n\nHowever, if the micro-bevel is too aggressive, it can lead to a \"goblin's grip\" on the wood, causing the chisel to bind and tear the fibers instead of cleanly cutting them. So, it's essential to strike a balance between a micro-bevel and a standard flat bevel.\n\nIn your case, since you're working with walnut, which can be a bit more challenging to cut, a micro-bevel on the tip of the chisel might be beneficial. However, I'd recommend starting with a very small micro-bevel (around 0.5-1 degree) and gradually increasing it as needed to achieve the desired cutting action.",
